WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:14.520
DNA origami is a method for making any shape or pattern that you might want 

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.470
out of DNA.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:20.810
So the way it works is that you begin with a very long single strand of 

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:21.220
DNA,

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:26.270
and the computer program will then generate a bunch of small DNA strands,

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.350
which we call staples.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:32.350
And so the net action of those 200 little short strands of DNA 

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.370
folds that long strand into whatever shape or pattern you want.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:41.540
So BIOMOD is a design competition for students 

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.070
and currently it's focused on college students.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.340
So all the teams come from all over the world and present their projects,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.650
and the idea is to give them hands-on experience in the lab.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.320
Since we're from Japan,

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.460
we wanted to do a Japanese origami using DNA origami. 

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:04.670
Gene expression mechanisms are controlled by thousands of transcription factors and some of these 

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.080
processes are essential for life,

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.460
but transcription factors can also cause diseases,

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.650
so we decided to develop a natural chemical DNA based biosensor that can 

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:18.510
be adapted to include the recognition sequence for many transcription factors.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.570
So we wanted to develop a solution towards one of the most prevalent issues facing our hospitals,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.280
which is antimicrobial resistance.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.610
It's in the headlines, particular in Australia, all the time where traditional drugs 

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:31.830
are not working as well,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:36.010
where the pathogens are evolving mechanisms against our normal drug therapies.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.620
So as a team we wanted to come up with a solution.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:43.690
We looked at the mechanism of cell lysis, puncturing a hole in the 

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:48.200
membranes of cells and releasing their contents. And our solution is a DNA 

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:53.190
origami nanobot that can puncture bacterial membranes and allow for 

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.150
its contents to spill out.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.140
So I think nano scale technology is a really awesome field because it's,

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:58.510
I think,

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:03.500
an area that has been under explored compared to other scientific fields. So it's a really hot area right now,

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.560
which is why I was really drawn to this,

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.140
the whole team was really drawn to this area.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:12.850
And in terms of why we wanted to use nano scale things in DNA origami,

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.060
I guess it's because of its self assembly, so you don't have to spend ages in a lab trying like synthesize 

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.220
something that has this exact structure.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:24.580
You can actually just program it and be lazy and on a computer. And then just chuck all the things together,

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.660
and it forms really nicely into what you want to do.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.430
So I think it's really awesome that it can do stuff like that.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:32.200
Which means that when it comes to scaling up things,

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.840
I guess it becomes easier.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:38.100
So if we use this as a direct delivery system,

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:42.320
we can possibly use this to cure some diseases like cancer.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.810
Taking it further into kind of the clinical world,

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:48.180
like seeing how we could try and deliver this to patients,

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.570
seeing how we could combat the fact that DNA is degraded in the human body.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:57.180
So looking at ways we could edit the DNA or protect it 

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:58.970
and those sorts of things.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.620
So really trying to apply it more into a clinical setting.

